sections.body:
OCD in men often looks different, and it often hides. Research finds that in men, OCD:

- **tends to start earlier**, often in childhood or the teen years;
- **more often involves sexual, religious, aggressive, or symmetry obsessions**, and rituals done silently in the mind;
- **can show up in the body first**, such as worries about urination or sexual function that lead to doctor visits before anyone names OCD;
- **is often kept secret**, because shame and the pressure to be strong and in control make it hard to ask for help.

The encouraging part: treatment works just as well for men as for women. Luke works with boys and men, 8 and up, and an unwanted thought never says anything about your character.

*Research: Mathes, Morabito & Schmidt (2019), Current Psychiatry Reports; Torresan et al. (2009), Comprehensive Psychiatry; de Mathis et al. (2011), Revista Brasileira de Psiquiatria; Conti, Fineberg et al. (2023), Trends in Urology & Men's Health; Goodman, Lochner & Stein (2021), Comprehensive Men's Mental Health; Surles (2025), International OCD Foundation.*
